 "genre_note": "c09 is not news. All 20 documents are Polygon service/enthusiast journalism from Nov-Dec 2023: buying guides, listicle roundups, game walkthroughs, reviews, and opinion essays. That changes the building-block mix sharply from what the news default predicts.\n\nBB distribution across 204 notes: concept ~127 (products, films, games, classes, TTRPGs \u2014 \"what is X?\"), empirical_observation ~52 (dated streaming premieres, deal prices, award wins, hardware specs and launch dates), argument ~19 (the four opinion pieces plus recommendation verdicts), procedure ~7 (the BG3 Shadow Curse walkthrough, controller-on-PC setup, price tracking, power-bank sizing), counter_argument 2, model 2. hypothesis: ZERO. No document in this cluster makes a testable prediction \u2014 the closest is the concert-film essay's claim that filmed concerts are \"the future of pop fandom\", which is a normative argument about the present, not a falsifiable forecast. navigation: none produced, as instructed. Absence of hypothesis is a real finding about the genre, not a gap in the decomposition.\n\nWhere coherence fought size. Three documents are pure roundups where the \"one document, fifteen unrelated items\" trap is live, and they got item-level notes rather than merged summaries: doc_0382 (15 separate film releases with their own director/cast/runtime/review), doc_0454 (13 unrelated home-theater products), doc_0591 (22 distinct TTRPGs, each praised by a different named contributor). doc_0392 is the hard case \u2014 225 blocks of a Netflix catalog listing where individual entries are 25-word publisher blurbs. One note per title would produce ~100 notes each too thin to retrieve on, and merging the whole thing gives a note retrieved for everything. I split it on the document's own organizing axis, availability date, into seven contiguous-date notes plus separate notes for the Netflix Games entries, the licensed library back-catalog (bare title lines with no descriptions \u2014 a genuinely different kind of content from the originals), the month overview, and the Social Network editor's pick (an argument, not a listing).\n\nShared entity notes (the multi-hop payload). Four titles appear in both doc_0382 and doc_0392 and got ONE note each drawing blocks from both: insidious_the_red_door, sly_sylvester_stallone_documentary, nyad_diana_nyad_biographical_drama, wingwomen_melanie_laurent_action_film. Two Baldur's Gate 3 subjects are split across doc_0016 and doc_0017 and got one note each: bg3_warlock_class_overview and bg3_warlock_subclasses_fiend_great_old_one_archfey. The verbatim \"there's no single best class\" paragraph appears in both BG3 articles and is one note, bg3_no_single_best_class_argument. Inside doc_0236, the 8BitDo USB Adapter 2 is pitched three times in different product sections and is one note, not three.\n\nDeliberate splits that resisted entity-merging. doc_0382's Across the Spider-Verse note (a dated Netflix debut, empirical_observation) and doc_0453's Miguel O'Hara note (a character's vampirism, concept) concern the same film but answer different questions under different building blocks, so rule 2 forces them apart. Same reasoning for Baldur's Gate 3, which appears in five documents: the class guide, the Warlock guide, the quest walkthrough, Astarion-as-surprise-vampire, and as a comparison point in the Rogue Trader review \u2014 each is a different subject, not a duplicate of one \"BG3\" entity.\n\nDropped and inspected. 141 blocks dropped, ~1,050 words: article H1s, \"New on Netflix\"/\"Available Nov. 14\" section headers, Polygon's What to Buy / What to Watch / What to Play / Polygon Recommends house promos, Vox Media affiliate disclosures, the vampire-week banner repeated in doc_0452 and doc_0453, \"Related:\" cross-links, and two price-check update stamps. I deliberately did NOT drop headers that carry a claim rather than a label: doc_0016's \"Best melee class for beginners: Fighter\", doc_0574's \"Most Black Friday return policies extend through January 2024\", doc_0452's \"Verdict: Vampire\" lines, and doc_0454's product names are all assigned, because each states something a reader would ask about. The two update notices (\"Checked prices and links for accuracy\") were read before dropping and add no scope condition. Coverage: every document lands 90.4% or above; the floor is doc_0519, where 91 of 952 words are affiliate boilerplate and house promo.\n\nVerified mechanically with scripts/verify_cluster_plan.py at --min-coverage 0.90: PASS on block ranges, no duplicate (doc, block), one BB per note, no note over the 1,800-word ceiling, all 26 sub-plans within 4-15 notes, all 204 filenames unique lowercase slugs. Quarantine held \u2014 only data/corpus/multihop_rag/*.txt and its index.json were read; MultiHopRAG.json and anything under data/raw/ were never opened."
